# Display companion runtime files
display/.token
display/stats.json
display/text_log.json
display/session_tail.json
display/app.log
display/app.pid
# The display can run on any port, and the pid file is named for it. Listing
# ports one at a time (app-5001.pid) meant a server on the next port left an
# untracked file that `git add -A` would happily commit. Glob it.
display/app-*.pid
display/cert.pem
display/key.pem
display/.campaign
display/.input_queue
display/.input_queue.tmp
display/.input_trigger
display/.autorun-session
display/.approved_devices.json
display/narration_target
display/roll_prefs.json
display/input_log.json
display/player_input.json
# GM-watcher runtime artefacts (B12: these were untracked and accumulating)
display/.gm-watch.log
display/.gm-watch.pid
display/.input_queue.taken

# Battle map artwork (third-party, 5-13MB each). The map JSONs in
# display/maps/ are committed and list normally; the pictures they point at are
# installed locally by scripts/art_import.py. A clone without them still loads
# every map, as bare terrain grids -- see display/maps/README.md.
display/maps/images/

# Token portraits (third-party, by hearden; 98 PNGs, 12MB). Same reasoning as
# the map artwork above, and the same precedent. The manifest that indexes them
# -- scripts/tactics/token_portraits.py -- IS committed, so a clone without the
# art still knows the set exists, still credits the artist, and still draws
# every token correctly from its side colour alone.
display/tokens/

# Generated by scripts/map_catalog.py and scripts/campus_extract.py. Both are
# rebuilt from local sources in a second; committing them would only create
# merge conflicts on a file nobody hand-edits.
docs/MAP-CATALOG.html
display/maps/images/.thumbs/

# SRD dataset (generated — run build_srd.py)
systems/dnd5e/data/dnd5e_srd.json
systems/dnd5e/data/dnd5e_supplemental.json

# The Atlas bestiary (generated — run export_bestiary.py --out .). 334 notes,
# 1.3MB, and the script's own words are that it is "DERIVED OUTPUT. It is
# regenerated wholesale and never read back by the engine". The scenes in
# atlas-vtt/ DO reference these notes by path, and that reference is a real
# file check on export rather than a guess — so a clone without them still has
# valid scene files, they just have no statblocks to open. Rebuild with:
#   python3 scripts/export_bestiary.py --out .
Bestiary/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
campaigns/

# Local-only helper files
no_think.md
paths.md

# OS
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
display/.scheme

# Probe run logs (verbose stdout — results are in .json)
probe/results/**/*.log
probe/results/*.log

# Draft docs (not ready to publish)
docs/DISCUSSION-DRAFT.md
docs/PR-DRAFT.md
display/.port
display/.preflight

# Chartdown tooling (scripts/chartdown_to_atlas.mjs); the Python engine does not need these
node_modules/
package-lock.json
